{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049392 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092185 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092025 :question/math-academy-id "e-4520" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+5y-2z &=-2 \\\\ -3y+z &=-2 \\\\ 3x-7y+3z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Given that $\\mathbf x = \\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n\n$$\n\\qquad A^{-1}= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=3, y=0, z=-2.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=3+0+(-2)=1.$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092817 :question/math-academy-id "q-78548" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y + 4z &=7 \\\\ x - y + z &=-1 \\\\ y - 2z &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax = b$. Given that $x = ⟨x, y, z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x + y + z$?\n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n$A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092818 :question/math-academy-id "q-78557" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z &=2 \\\\ x-2y-5z &=-1 \\\\ 2x-3y+2z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-19 & -7 & 11 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-19 & -7 & 11 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=5,y=3,z=0.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=5+3+0=8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292892 :question/math-academy-id "q-275781" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+z &=-1 \\\\ x-y+3z &=2 \\\\ -2x+y-z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, and the inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe solution of the system of equations is \n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, \n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}, \n$\\,z=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is \n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=0,\\,z=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348080 :question/math-academy-id "q-78421" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y+3z &=0 \\\\ 8x-3y+5z &=1 \\\\ 7x-2y+4z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=1,y=-1,z=-2.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=1+(-1)+(-2)=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348093 :question/math-academy-id "q-78550" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y-2z &=-2 \\\\ 5x+y+z &=-1 \\\\ -8x+2y+z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -13 & -15 & -11 \\\\ 18 & 22 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -13 & -15 & -11 \\\\ 18 & 22 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=0,y=4,z=-5.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=0+4+(-5)=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348094 :question/math-academy-id "q-78551" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=1 \\\\ 3x+2y-z &=1 \\\\ x-4y+3z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -10 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -14 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -10 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -14 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=1,y=-2,z=-2.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=1+(-2)+(-2)=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348095 :question/math-academy-id "q-78554" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z &=0 \\\\ x+3y+z &=-1 \\\\ 2x-3y-z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $\\frac{yz}{x}?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=-1,y=-1,z=3.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{yz}{x}=\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 3}{-1}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348096 :question/math-academy-id "q-78555" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x-y-z &=3 \\\\ 6x-3y-5z &=5 \\\\ 5x-y-2z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -13 & 9 & -16 \\\\ 9 & -7 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -13 & 9 & -16 \\\\ 9 & -7 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=0,y=-5,z=2.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=0+(-5)+2=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393877 :question/math-academy-id "q-78549" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+4z &=-2 \\\\ x+3y+9z &=0 \\\\ y+6z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=0,y=-3,z=1.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=0+(-3)+1=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393884 :question/math-academy-id "q-78552" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-3y+2z &=-4 \\\\ -4x+4y-3z &=7 \\\\ x-y+z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=1,y=2,z=-1.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=1+2+(-1)=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435149 :question/math-academy-id "q-78553" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z &=3 \\\\ x-2y+z &=1 \\\\ 2x-y+2z &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=-1,y=1,z=4.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=-1+1+4=4.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Given the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092186 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092026 :question/math-academy-id "e-4521" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations using matrices:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3y+x+z &=0 \\\\ z+4y-5 &=2 \\\\ 1+2x-y &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are in the correct order and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y+z &=0 \\\\ 0x+4y+z &=7 \\\\ 2x-y+0z &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nNow, the solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we compute $A^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1*: Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)-3(-2)+(-8) \\\\  & =1+6-8 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2*: Compute the matrix of minors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3*:  Find the matrix of cofactors.\n\n\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\n*Step 4*: Transpose the matrix of cofactors.\n\n\n\n$$\nC^T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\n*Step 5*: Apply the inverse matrix formula.\n\n\n\n$$\nA^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^T=\\dfrac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix} =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -4\\end{bmatrix} .\n$$\n\n\n\nFinally, we can calculate the solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -33\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3,$ $y=10,$ $z=-33.$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092709 :question/math-academy-id "q-42449" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-3+z &=0 \\\\ 5y-1+3x &=2z+1 \\\\ 3z+2x-1 &=2y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind  $x+y+z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+z &=3 \\\\ 3x+5y-2z &=2 \\\\ 2x-2y+3z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-0+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(15-4)+(-6-10) \\\\  & =22-16 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 13 & -16 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -13 & -16 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=4-4-5=-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092937 :question/math-academy-id "q-9722" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2y+3z+x &=-1 \\\\ 4z+y &=1 \\\\ 5x+6y-3 &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z &=-1 \\\\ y+4z &=1 \\\\ 5x+6y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-24)-2(-20)+3(-5) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & -20 & -5 \\\\ -18 & -15 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 20 & -5 \\\\ 18 & -15 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}42 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=42,y=-35,z=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237787 :question/math-academy-id "q-10038" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}z+2y+7x &=1 \\\\ 3y-z &=-2 \\\\ 4y-2z-3x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7x+2y+z &=1 \\\\ 3y-z &=-2 \\\\ -3x+4y-2z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7(-6+4)-2(-3)+1(9) \\\\  & =-14+6+9 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 9 \\\\ -8 & -11 & 34 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 8 & -11 & -34 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 77\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-18,\\,y=25,\\,z=77.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237803 :question/math-academy-id "q-10074" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2y+3z &=-1-x \\\\ 4y &=-5z \\\\ x+6z-3 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z &=-1 \\\\ 4y+5z &=0 \\\\ x+6z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(24)-2(-5)+3(0-4) \\\\  & =24+10-12 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 12 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 5 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{15}{11} \\\\ -\\frac{10}{11} \\\\ \\frac{8}{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-\\frac{15}{11},y=-\\frac{10}{11},z=\\frac{8}{11}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237804 :question/math-academy-id "q-10075" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3z-y+x-5 &=0 \\\\ y+2x+2z &=1 \\\\ z-2x-2y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z &=5 \\\\ 2x+y+2z &=1 \\\\ -2x-2y+z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1+4)+1(2+4)+3(-4+2) \\\\  & =5+6-6 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -\\frac{19}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=3,y=-\\frac{19}{5},z=-\\frac{3}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237805 :question/math-academy-id "q-10076" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y+3z &=0 \\\\ 8x-3y+5z &=9 \\\\ 7x-2y+4z &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(-12+10)+2(32-35)+3(-16+21) \\\\  & =-8-6+15 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ -49 \\\\ -58\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=19,y=-49,z=-58.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237810 :question/math-academy-id "q-10100" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+z &=6 \\\\ 4x+7y+2z &=0 \\\\ 3x+y+z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(7-2)-3(-2)+1(4-21) \\\\  & =10+6-17 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & -17 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -17 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -17 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -17 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 17 & -7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 17 & -7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-29 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 100\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-29,y=-12,z=100.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237812 :question/math-academy-id "q-10101" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-z &=0 \\\\ x+y+3z &=2 \\\\ 3x-2y+z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1-3(-2))+0-1(-2-3) \\\\  & =1+6+2+3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -8 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=\\frac{2}{3},y=-\\frac{2}{3},z=\\frac{2}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237814 :question/math-academy-id "q-10102" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2y+2z &=1 \\\\ x+y+z &=3 \\\\ y+2z &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2(2)+2(1) \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 4 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{2},y=4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240893 :question/math-academy-id "q-104536" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+z &=1 \\\\ 2x-2z &=2+2y \\\\ 2y+4z &=2x-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind  $x+y+z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+z &=1 \\\\ 2x-2y-2z &=2 \\\\ -2x+2y+4z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-(8-4)+(4-4) \\\\  & =-4+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{-4}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=2+1+0=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240894 :question/math-academy-id "q-104541" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+z+x &=2 \\\\ -3z+y &=3 \\\\ 2x+y &=4-4z\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=2 \\\\ y-3z &=3 \\\\ 2x+y+4z &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4+3)-(0+6)+(0-2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=11,y=-6,z=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240895 :question/math-academy-id "q-104546" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}z+x &=2 \\\\ 2y-3z &=1 \\\\ 2z-y &=-3x+3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+z &=2 \\\\ 2y-3z &=1 \\\\ 3x-y+2z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4-3)-0+(0-6) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -9 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -9 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -9 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -9 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=1,y=2,z=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292893 :question/math-academy-id "q-275787" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+z &=4x+y+2 \\\\ x+y &=2z+2 \\\\ y &=x-y+z+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $x=[x,y,z]^{T}$ of the system by computing the inverse of the coefficient matrix.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x-y+z &=2 \\\\ x+y-2z &=2 \\\\ -x+2y-z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(-1+4)+(-1-2)+(2+1) \\\\  & =-9-3+3 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415690 :question/math-academy-id "q-10091" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+2z &=0 \\\\ x+2y+3z &=2 \\\\ 3x+y+z &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-1(1-9)+2(1-6) \\\\  & =+8-10 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -8 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 9 \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{2},y=9,z=-\\frac{9}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435147 :question/math-academy-id "q-104515" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z &=-1 \\\\ 3x-z &=5+3y \\\\ 3z-2y &=6-x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind  $x+y+z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z &=-1 \\\\ 3x-3y-z &=5 \\\\ x-2y+3z &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-9-2)-(9+1)-2(-6+3) \\\\  & =-22-10+6 \\\\  & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 10 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & -10 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 5 \\\\ -7 & -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-26 \\\\ 26 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=1-1+1=1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations By Calculating the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092187 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092027 :question/math-academy-id "e-4604" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation $B\\,\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\,\\mathbf{d},$ where $A$ and $B$ are $10 \\times 10$ non-singular matrices, while $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are $10 \\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $\\mathbf{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse $B^{−1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $B\\,\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\,\\mathbf{d}$ by $B^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {B}^{-1}B\\,x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{10}\\,x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092813 :question/math-academy-id "q-78442" :question/problem "Consider a system of $3$ linear equations in $3$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,y=w.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $y$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $w$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,y & =w \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,y & =w \\\\ {I}_{3}\\,y & ={D}^{-1}\\,w \\\\  & ={D}^{-1}\\,w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and left to right, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" :question/worked-solution "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,y=w$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,y & =w \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,y & ={D}^{-1}w \\\\ {I}_{3}\\,y & ={D}^{-1}\\,w \\\\ y & ={D}^{-1}\\,w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and $y.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092819 :question/math-academy-id "q-78572" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation ${A}^{-1}\\,x={B}^{-1}\\,d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $5\\times 5$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $5\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying both sides of the equation ${A}^{-1}\\,x={B}^{-1}\\,d$ by $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}\\,x & ={B}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ A{A}^{-1}\\,x & =A{B}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{5}\\,x & =A{B}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ x & =A{B}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299656 :question/math-academy-id "q-317900" :question/problem "Consider a system of $3$ linear equations in $3$ variables, written in matrix form as $A\\,x=b.$ Here, $A$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $x$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $b$ is the column vector containing the constants. What are the missing parts of the derivation method shown below to find the unique solution of the system?\n\n$$\nA\\,x={{field-1}}\n$$\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,b\n$$\n\n$$\n{I}_{3}\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={A}^{-1}\\,b\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${A}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $A\\,x=b$ by ${A}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,x & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}A\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b \\\\ {I}_{3}\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348097 :question/math-academy-id "q-78563" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation $A\\,x=B\\,d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $5\\times 5$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $5\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${A}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $A\\,x=B\\,d$ by ${A}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,x & =B\\,d \\\\ {A}^{-1}A\\,x & ={A}^{-1}B\\,d \\\\ {I}_{5}\\,x & ={A}^{-1}B\\,d \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}B\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348098 :question/math-academy-id "q-78569" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation ${B}^{-1}\\,x=A\\,d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $9\\times 9$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $9\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying both sides of the equation ${B}^{-1}\\,x=A\\,d$ by $B,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1}\\,x & =A\\,d \\\\ B{B}^{-1}\\,x & =BA\\,d \\\\ {I}_{9}\\,x & =BA\\,d \\\\ x & =BA\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348099 :question/math-academy-id "q-78570" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation ${A}^{-1}\\,x=B\\,d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $7\\times 7$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $7\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying both sides of the equation ${A}^{-1}\\,x=B\\,d$ by $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}\\,x & =B\\,d \\\\ A{A}^{-1}\\,x & =AB\\,d \\\\ {I}_{7}\\,x & =AB\\,d \\\\ x & =AB\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348100 :question/math-academy-id "q-78571" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation $A\\,x={B}^{-1}\\,d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $2\\times 2$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${A}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $A\\,x={B}^{-1}\\,d$ by ${A}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,x & ={B}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {A}^{-1}A\\,x & ={A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{2}\\,x & ={A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393856 :question/math-academy-id "q-78445" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation $AB\\,x=d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $4\\times 4$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $4\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${A}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $AB\\,x=d$ by ${A}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB\\,x & =d \\\\ {A}^{-1}AB\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{4}B\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ B\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${B}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $B\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,d$ by ${B}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {B}^{-1}B\\,x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{4}\\,x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393891 :question/math-academy-id "q-78566" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation ${A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,x=d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $3\\times 3$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying both sides of the equation ${A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,x=d$ by $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,x & =d \\\\ A{A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,x & =A\\,d \\\\ {I}_{3}{B}^{-1}\\,x & =A\\,d \\\\ {B}^{-1}\\,x & =A\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation ${B}^{-1}\\,x=A\\,d$ by $B,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1}\\,x & =A\\,d \\\\ B{B}^{-1}\\,x & =BA\\,d \\\\ {I}_{3}\\,x & =BA\\,d \\\\ x & =BA\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393898 :question/math-academy-id "q-78567" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation ${A}^{-1}B\\,x=d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $6\\times 6$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $6\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying both sides of the equation ${A}^{-1}B\\,x=d$ by $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}B\\,x & =d \\\\ A{A}^{-1}B\\,x & =A\\,d \\\\ {I}_{6}B\\,x & =A\\,d \\\\ B\\,x & =A\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${B}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $B\\,x=A\\,d$ by ${B}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\,x & =A\\,d \\\\ {B}^{-1}B\\,x & ={B}^{-1}A\\,d \\\\ {I}_{6}\\,x & ={B}^{-1}A\\,d \\\\ x & ={B}^{-1}A\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393905 :question/math-academy-id "q-78568" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation $A{B}^{-1}\\,x=d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $5\\times 5$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $5\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${A}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $A{B}^{-1}\\,x=d$ by ${A}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{B}^{-1}\\,x & =d \\\\ {A}^{-1}A{B}^{-1}\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{5}{B}^{-1}\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {B}^{-1}\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation ${B}^{-1}\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,d$ by $B,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1}\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ B{B}^{-1}\\,x & =B{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{5}\\,x & =B{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ x & =B{A}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403947 :question/math-academy-id "q-78559" :question/problem "Consider a system of $n$ linear equations in $n$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,z=c.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $z$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $c$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,z & =c \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,z & =\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{{D}^{-1}}}}c \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{{I}_{n}}}}z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c \\\\ z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and right to left, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" :question/worked-solution "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,z=c$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,z & =c \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,z & ={D}^{-1}c \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c \\\\ z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and ${I}_{n}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403954 :question/math-academy-id "q-78561" :question/problem "Consider a system of $n$ linear equations in $n$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,t=q.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $t$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $q$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,t & =q \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{{D}^{-1}}}}D\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\color{blue}{\\phantom{\\mathbf{q}}}} \\\\ t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and left to right, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" :question/worked-solution "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,t=q$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,t & =q \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q \\\\ t & ={D}^{-1}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and $q.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435148 :question/math-academy-id "q-78565" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation $B\\,x=A\\,d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $2\\times 2$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${B}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $B\\,x=A\\,d$ by ${B}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\,x & =A\\,d \\\\ {B}^{-1}B\\,x & ={B}^{-1}A\\,d \\\\ {I}_{2}\\,x & ={B}^{-1}A\\,d \\\\ x & ={B}^{-1}A\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Matrix Equation Using Algebraic Manipulation"}] :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations Using Inverse Matrices"}
